.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Huh
and
.I Crux
show the text of the
.IR acme (1)
window
.B $winid
by what a language model makes of it: how many bits each token costs, from
.IR lmfs ,
or
.IR lmsim (4)
in tests, mounted on
.I lmdir
.RB ( /mnt/lm ).
If
.I lmdir
has no
.BR clone ,
it mounts
.B /srv/lm
there, as
.B lmsim
.B -s
.B lm
posts it, since acme runs a command in acme's own namespace.
Each is meant to be run from the window's tag.
Each follows the window by its
.BR rdevent ,
so it leaves the window's
.B event
to acme or another program, and both run on one window at once, each by itself: neither reads
the other's output or knows its name.
.I Huh
highlights the stretches where the text's information is, those that cost the most;
.I Crux
draws the whole text as a strip in a
.IR rio (1)
window of its own, each token colored by the same score
.I Huh
highlights by, so the strip's hottest parts are
.IR Huh 's
highlights.
.PP
Each opens the window's
.B rdevent
file from the window's
.BR seq ,
and then reads its body under the same
.BR seq ,
so that no edit falls between.
It writes the text to a session of
.IR lmdir 's
.B data
file, after a preamble, the window's name as its tag has it, a colon and a blank line, as
.IR bits (1)
writes a file's, and leaves out the answer's lines for the preamble; a window with no name gets
none.
Then it follows the window's edits, by the
.B I
and
.B D
events, in a copy of the body: after each one it writes the whole copy to
.B data
on a new open and closes the old one, which drops what was left of it, and marks or draws again
from the answer.
.PP
.I Huh
writes a layer of
.IR astyles (4)
on the window,
.BR huh ,
under
.IR astyles 's
.B seq
guard, so a layer from an older text is not kept.
A
.I Huh
that starts on a window whose layers already include
.B huh
deletes that layer and its own, and exits; the
.I Huh
whose layer is gone sees so within a quarter second and ends.
So the word in the tag turns it on and off.
.PP
.I Huh
highlights, in the color
.BR huh ,
the stretches that
.I huh
prints, below, at its defaults, and segments nothing.
What it lights is where the model spends its bits: names, terms, code and figures, more than a
sentence that is merely new to the text.
It is meant for prose; code mostly falls under the floor, below.
Once a text is first scored and has been idle for a second,
.I Huh
holds its cutoffs and remembers its highlights.
After an edit, a run of tokens above the leaving cutoff stays highlighted while it overlaps a
highlight from before, and a new one enters only with a token above the cutoff for 0.7 times
the density, so a highlight away from the edit stays as it was.
When the text has again been idle for a second with the whole answer in,
.I Huh
fits the cutoffs again, and highlights afresh as
.I huh
does, only when the runes above the held cutoff differ from the density's share by more than
10% of it and by more than 128 runes.
While a text is first scored, the cutoffs come from the tokens scored so far;
Get, a new text and a change of density start afresh.
.PP
The density, in percent, from 5 to 30, sets the cutoff a highlight enters by, 10 by default; with
the leaving cutoff and whole words, the highlights cover a little more of a dense text, about 13%
of the tree's docs, and less of a bland one.
.B $home/lib/huh
holds the user's choice, a line
.BI density " n"\f1,
over the default.
.I Huh+
and
.IR Huh- ,
links to
.IR Huh ,
raise and lower it by 5, write
.BR $home/lib/huh
and print the new density,
as
.I Huh
with a
.I density
does, swept in the tag as
.BR "Huh 15" ;
they need no window.
A running
.I Huh
reads the file again when it changes, and marks again by the new density at once.
.PP
.IR Huh 's
color is a tint it gives in its layer's own color line, with an opacity, so that
.IR astyles (4)
blends it over the text's other colors in either theme.
.PP
.I Crux
is a script.
It runs
.IR crux ,
an ordinary
.I rio
program, on the window
.BR $winid ,
in a window of its own that
.IR window (1)
makes at the top right of acme's, by its old form with a rectangle, which mounts
.B $wsys
in the caller's name space, so that
.I crux
still has acme's
.BR /mnt/acme .
The script takes acme's
.B /dev
binding away first, whose empty
.B draw
keeps programs acme runs from drawing, and sends
.IR crux 's
diagnostics to acme's
.BR +Errors .
.I Crux
adds no layer to the text's window.
.PP
.IR Crux 's
window has a tag that looks and works as acme's
.RI ( adraw (2)),
named for the file with
.BR +Crux ,
and is labeled
.B crux
and the file.
It shows the text as a strip: a row for each of its lines, 6 pixels high, and a cell 4 pixels wide
for each rune that is not blank, colored by the heat of its token, both scaled up on a screen
of high resolution.
Once it has the text,
.I crux
makes its window as wide as the longest line needs and as tall as the text, below its tag,
whose lines wrap to show every word, within the screen
.I rio
manages, by a
.B resize
of its
.B /dev/wctl
.RI ( rio (4)),
and leaves its size alone after that.
The strip's cells keep their size whatever the window's: a text taller than the window scrolls, by
a scroll bar at the window's left that works as acme's, button 1 moving back, button 2 to the
place clicked and button 3 forward, and by the wheel, a sixth of the rows a turn, and the
lines past the window's right edge are cut.
A resize shows more or less of the strip, from the same row.
.PP
A token's heat is its smoothed score, as
.I huh
computes it below, placed on a ramp from 0 to 1.
The scale is relative by default: the score less the text's median score, over 1.5 times the
spread of its scores, their standard deviation weighted by runes and at least 0.1 bits a rune,
so a uniform text stays cool and its highest scores are hottest.
.B Abs
in
.IR crux 's
tag makes the scale absolute, 0 to 3 bits a rune in every text;
.B Rel
makes it relative again.
.B Heat
turns the heat off, leaving the text's shape in the theme's
.B comment
color, and on again.
The heat is blurred a little, across a row more than between rows, so it fades at a token's edge;
clicks still land on exact tokens.
The ramp is matplotlib's inferno, which keeps equal steps of score equal steps of color.
Its cold end is the theme's
.B body
background, tinted toward the
.B comment
color where there is text; on a dark theme it runs up inferno to pale yellow, and on a light one
it runs the other way in lightness, through yellow, orange and red to deep purple, so the hottest
text is the darkest.
Until the text is scored, and while it is first scored for the rest of it, the text is drawn in
its cold color.
.PP
A mark between the scroll bar and the strip shows the lines of the text window's dot, which
.B rdevent
reports as
.BR MS ;
when dot moves out of the rows the strip shows, the strip scrolls to keep it in view, a third of
the way down.
Button 1 on the strip selects its token in the text's window and shows it.
Button 3 on the strip writes the token's line to
.BR +Errors ,
as
.I Huh
.B -t
does, below, with its smoothed score after its bits.
.PP
The program reaches the window's files through
.IR acme (2).
Acme handles every event in the text's window itself; the program only watches them.
After a
.BR Get ,
it reads the reloaded body and marks or draws it again.
When the window is deleted its
.B rdevent
ends, and so does the program.
However
.I Huh
ends, by the window's deletion, a failure of
.IR lmdir ,
its layer's deletion or a note, it deletes its layer.
Del or
.B Delete
in
.IR crux 's
tag ends
.IR crux ,
and the text window stays; deleting its window in
.I rio
ends it the same way.
.BR Put ,
.B Undo
and
.B Redo
there are the text window's, and the tag and label follow the text window's name.
.I Crux
run where a window that
.I rio
labels
.B crux
and the text window's file shows deletes that window and exits, so
.B Crux
in the text window's tag turns it on and off.
.PP
.PP
.B Huh
.B -t
writes the alternatives for the token at the window's dot to
.B +Errors
and exits, in any window, with or without
.I Huh
or
.IR Crux ;
.B cmd-k
.B h
in
.B /lib/hotkey/acme
runs it on the window with the focus.
The line gives the token's address in the file, the token, its bits and rank, and the model's
five likeliest tokens in its place, from
.IR lmdir 's
.BR top ,
each with its bits; button 3 on the line shows the token.
The alternatives tell a misspelling, whose likeliest token is the right spelling, from a new
idea.
.PP
The command
.I huh
scores each
.IR file ,
or its standard input, once, and prints a line for each highlighted stretch:
.IP
.EX
README.md:#2210,#2398 3.41
.EE
.LP
its address and its score.
A file goes to the model after its preamble, as in
.IR bits (1),
and its addresses are
.IR file :# q0 ,# q1 .
Standard input has no name, so it gets no preamble and its addresses are
.RI # q0 ,# q1
alone;
.B -n
gives it
.I name
for both.
.PP
Each token's bits are clipped at
.I bits
(15 by default) and spread over its runes, less, in a text's first 512 tokens, a position
baseline: the bits a rune the first tokens of the tree's docs cost beyond those later in them,
since a text's start costs more for lack of context even after its preamble.
Each token's score is the clipped bits over the runes of a window of
.I width
tokens around it (32 by default), weighted by a triangle that falls off from the token.
The cutoff is the score above which the tokens cover
.I density
percent of the text's runes, 10 by default; unlike
.IR Huh ,
.I huh
reads no file.
The cutoffs stop at a floor of 1.7 bits a rune, near the 90th percentile of the scores over the
tree's docs, man pages and C, so a bland text gets less than its share, or nothing, and a text
under twice
.I width
tokens gets nothing.
A stretch is a run of tokens above the cutoff for 1.5 times the density that holds a token above
the cutoff for the density, and is at least
.I tokens
tokens long (4 by default);
.B -l
.B 0
keeps every one.
A stretch's ends move out to the blanks around the words they fall in, up to 32 runes, so a
highlight holds whole words, and stretches that then meet are one.
A stretch's score is its tokens' scores, weighted by their runes.
With
.BR -a ,
.I huh
prints every stretch, a run of tokens above the median score, highlighted or not.
Nothing is parsed: the text is plain text to the model, and every stretch comes from the tokens'
bits and their places.
.PP
.I Crux
.B -l
prints, as text, the heat the strip draws: a line for each line of each
.IR file ,
or of its standard input, with its address, the mean and the largest of the smoothed score over
the tokens in the line, the mean weighted by runes, and the line's heat, that of its hottest
token, on the relative scale:
.IP
.EX
README.md:#2210,#2291 2.95 3.41 0.97
.EE
.LP
A line's address takes in its newline, and the score is the one
.I crux
draws by.
Its preamble and addresses are as
.IR huh 's,
and
.B -n
names standard input the same way.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/hotkey/acme
.TP
.B $home/lib/huh
the user's density, written by
.I Huh+
and
.IR Huh-
.TP
.B /lib/hotkey/acme
.B cmd-k
.B h
.PD
.SH EXAMPLES
In a window's tag, with
.IR lmsim (4)
or an
.I lmfs
on
.BR /mnt/lm :
.IP
.EX
Huh+ Huh- Huh Crux
.EE
.LP
The lines of a file whose heat passes 0.9:
.IP
.EX
crux -l README.md | awk '$4 > 0.9'
.EE
